WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.200
All right, let's get into audience&nbsp;
questions for the week. First one is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.880
David, based on the numbers we're seeing&nbsp;
about loyalty to Donald Trump and people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:10.160
willing to leave the Republican&nbsp;
Party if he starts a new party.&nbsp;

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:15.920
Is it fair to say that Donald Trump's voters are&nbsp;
more loyal to him than Obama and Biden's voters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:21.200
are loyal to them? I think that there's&nbsp;
a complexity to this question, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:29.840
what we mean by loyalty may not be the same&nbsp;
thing. If what we mean by loyalty, we mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:35.520
lack of willingness to criticize them.&nbsp;
In other words, you're loyal in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:41.440
you'll never criticize anything they do. I&nbsp;
think that, yes, Donald Trump's followers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:49.360
are more loyal to him. You see, many on the left&nbsp;
who vote for voted for Biden, voted for Obama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:55.040
voted for Obama for reelection, and yet had many&nbsp;
criticisms of them. And some might say that that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:00.480
means they're less loyal. I don't know. I'm not&nbsp;
so sure about that. I think that one of the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:07.680
that does that is unique to the Trump voter is&nbsp;
that because many of them either were sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:13.760
like disaffected Republicans who already had some&nbsp;
disdain for the Republican Party or were brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:19.280
into politics by Trump. And if Trump had been&nbsp;
a Democrat, they would have become Democrats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.920
Trump ran as a Republican. So there, you&nbsp;
know, putatively ostensibly Republicans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:30.800
they don't really care about the Republican Party.&nbsp;
And when you think about all of these folks who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:39.840
joined politics essentially to follow Donald&nbsp;
Trump, they liked the dog whistling on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:46.320
xenophobic sort of racist stuff. They don't really&nbsp;
care about abortion. Like, you know, Trump made up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.560
this thing about how he used to be pro-choice. Now&nbsp;
he's pro-life because he made a kitty like there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:55.760
something like that at 68 and then he became&nbsp;
pro-life. And so that appealed to some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:00.800
evangelicals. Most historical Republicans went&nbsp;
with Trump because they weren't going to go for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:05.840
Hillary. But the people that Trump brought in,&nbsp;
they don't really care about most of the stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:09.680
that a lot of Republicans care about. They'll go&nbsp;
with it if Trump wants it. But they're not really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:14.240
about the debt. They're not really about abortion.&nbsp;
They're not really about religious liberty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:19.920
They liked the sort of vibe of Trump and the&nbsp;
dog whistling that came with it. Trump came in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.800
and he said, you know, George W. Bush was wrong&nbsp;
on the war and Mitt Romney sucks. And, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:29.440
John McCain. I prefer war heroes who don't get&nbsp;
captured and Obama might not even be American and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:34.320
Mexican rapists and all this different&nbsp;
stuff. And that was really what they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:41.680
motivated by. And so it's less to me that they&nbsp;
are super loyal to Trump. It's that they don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:45.600
really care about anything else that's going on in&nbsp;
the political world. They never really cared about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:50.320
anything other than Trump's words. Even Trump's&nbsp;
actions they don't really care about. Remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:54.160
about seven or eight months into Trump's&nbsp;
presidency, I got a call from some guy in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:58.880
California, I think it was, who said, you know,&nbsp;
Trump's just the best. Everything he's done.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:03.520
I said, well, what has he actually done so&nbsp;
far? And he didn't actually list anything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:07.920
And I said, well, what motivated you to vote for&nbsp;
Trump? What promises did you like? And he said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:13.200
building the wall. I said, OK, so if there's&nbsp;
no wall across the entire border in November&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.640
of twenty twenty, you're not voting for Trump. And&nbsp;
he said, no, I'm probably still voting for Trump.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:23.360
They don't really care about issues. They&nbsp;
care about Trump because of the things he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:30.880
says and how he says them. And in that way, it's a&nbsp;
debate, whether you call it loyalty or simply they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:37.280
don't care enough about anything else to consider&nbsp;
abandoning Donald Trump. I think it's the latter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:41.520
You tell me whether the term&nbsp;
loyalty does or doesn't apply

